1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.100
Filters help you focus on exactly the data that matters. 

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.800
Let's explore how to use them effectively

3
00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:09.366
and what limits to watch out for. 

4
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.466
Filters let you narrow down your report to specific criteria.

5
00:00:13.966 --> 00:00:14.966
For example, 

6
00:00:14.966 --> 00:00:17.132
if you're analyzing screen time, 

7
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:21.799
you can set a filter to only show apps with more than 500 minutes of usage. 

8
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.966
This helps you focus on the apps that matter most.

9
00:00:25.766 --> 00:00:27.132
Here's something important.

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.800
Line and pie charts often require filters.

11
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.600
If a filter is required, you'll see a notification in the filter field.

12
00:00:35.966 --> 00:00:39.499
Tables on the other hand, make filters optional. 

13
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:42.399
Be aware of filter limits.

14
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.660
Depending on your report type and the data you're filtering, 

15
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.674
there may be a maximum number of items you can select. 

16
00:00:48.674 --> 00:00:53.299
For example, a line chart filtering by app name might limit you to 10 apps. 

17
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.900
Tables typically allow more, up to 100 in some cases.

18
00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:02.366
If you hit a limit, don't worry. 

19
00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:04.932
Just click the X next to a filter to remove it

20
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.499
Filters are powerful,

21
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.900
but sometimes things don't go as planned.

22
00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:12.349
In our final video, 

23
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.233
we'll cover common issues and how to resolve them quickly.

24
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:16.466
Let's wrap this up!

